<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guadiana</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Guadiana is a river of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, about 750 km long, flowing west through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-mancha">La Mancha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/extremadura">Extremadura</a> past Mérida and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a>, then south to form part of the Spanish–Portuguese border before reaching the Atlantic. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> called it the Anas, and it separated Lusitania from Baetica. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its middle course was the heart of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> of Badajoz.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guadiana.jpg" alt="Guadiana" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco Antunes · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guadiana_river.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
